---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Grain from "../../components/Grain.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Grain"
  description="A falling-sand sandbox on a chunked cellular automaton: paint sand, water, fire and acid, and only the chunks that move cost anything."
>
  <div class="gr">
    <section class="gr-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">playground</p>
      <h1>Grain, a falling-sand sandbox</h1>
      <p class="gr-lede">
        Paint sand, water, fire, acid, gas, oil, wood and stone, drop spawners
        that keep pouring, and watch them interact. Click inside the sandbox
        first and the keyboard shortcuts work too: <kbd>1</kbd>–<kbd>9</kbd> for
        elements, <kbd>Space</kbd> to pause, <kbd>V</kbd> to show chunks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Grain height="min(82vh, 760px)" />

    <article class="gr-article">
      <h2>A grid of cells with simple rules</h2>
      <p>
        The world is a cellular automaton: a grid where every cell holds one
        element, and each step every cell follows a few local rules. Sand tries
        to fall straight down, then diagonally. Water does the same but also
        spreads sideways. Heavier elements sink through lighter ones, which is
        why sand settles under water and oil floats on top of it. Fire burns
        down on its own and spreads to wood, oil and gas. Acid eats what it
        touches and is diluted by water.
      </p>

      <h2>Only the parts that move</h2>
      <p>
        Most of a sandbox is still. A settled sand pile doesn't need to be
        looked at sixty times a second, so the grid is split into 16×16 chunks.
        Any cell that changes wakes its chunk for the next step, and its
        neighbour too when the cell sits on an edge. A step only visits
        chunks that were woken, and the rest sleep and cost nothing.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`export function step() {
  emit();
  tick = (tick + 1) & 255;
  const t = act; act = nxt; nxt = t; nxt.fill(0);
  for (let cy = CH - 1; cy >= 0; cy--) {
    // skip whole rows of sleeping chunks, then
    // sweep the awake ones bottom-up
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Two details keep it honest. Rows are swept from the bottom up, and each
        row alternates left-to-right and right-to-left so liquids don't drift
        to one side. Every cell also records the tick it last moved on, so a
        grain that falls into a row that hasn't been visited yet isn't moved a
        second time in the same step.
      </p>
      <p>
        Turn on <em>Show chunks</em> in the panel to see it. Pour some water
        and the highlighted squares follow the flow, then go out one by one as
        it settles.
      </p>

      <h2>The source</h2>
      <p>
        Plain HTML, CSS and ES modules with no dependencies and no build step:
        about five hundred lines across the element tables, the engine and the
        UI. Read it at <a class="link" href="https://github.com/swayam5342/grain">github.com/swayam5342/grain</a>,
        or <a class="link" href="/grain/">open the sandbox on its own page</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .gr {
    max-width: 70rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 1.5rem 4rem;
  }
  .gr code,
  .gr kbd {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
  }

  .gr-intro {
    padding: 3rem 0 1.5rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .gr-intro h1 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0.8rem;
  }
  .gr-lede {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .gr-article {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
  .gr-article h2 {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    margin: 2.4em 0 0.7em;
  }
  .gr-article h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .gr-article p {
    margin: 0 0 1.2em;
  }
  .gr-article pre {
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0 0 1.2em;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .gr {
      padding: 0 1rem 3rem;
    }
    .gr-intro {
      padding-top: 2rem;
    }
  }
</style>
